DO NOT read any files or run any commands — answer purely from this prompt; everything you need is inline. Be concise and direct.
Adversarial review, answer in <=20 lines: VERDICT (SHIP / DON'T SHIP) + numbered findings with the concrete failure each causes.

System: a phone app lists a Claude Code session's subagents via a desk API (`GET /subagents`). Each row has `state` in {finished, running, stalled, unknown} derived from the parent transcript (a `completed` record wins), else from the agent transcript's mtime (running if written within 15 min, stalled otherwise). Yesterday's change added "the desk pressed x and saw the row vanish" as a memory that reported `finished` + reason `stopped-by-desk`; a reviewer objected that `finished` reads as successful completion.

Change under review:
1. A fifth state `stopped` (display "Stopped"), counted in `counts.stopped` (the counts object gains a key; the phone model declares it optional so older desks still decode).
2. Two sources: (a) the parent transcript's `<task-notification>` with `<status>killed</status>` — measured on the production desk today: Claude Code 2.1.263 writes exactly this with summary 'Agent "…" was stopped by user' when the user (or the desk pressing x) stops an agent; natural completion writes `completed`; a killed background shell writes `failed` (ignored here). Reason `stopped-by-user`. (b) the desk's own memory of an observed stop while the agent transcript stays silent (+5 s grace), reason `stopped-by-desk`.
3. Precedence: completed > killed > (parent unreadable → unknown) > desk memory > mtime. A killed agent can be resumed ("send it another message"), after which a later `completed` record makes it finished; the notification note says the same task-id may notify more than once.
4. The listing note gains "N stopped". Display text for finished stays "Finished".

Questions:
- Precedence: completed beats killed regardless of order in the transcript (sets, not sequence). If an agent is killed AFTER completing (possible? e.g. resumed then killed again), the row would say finished. Is that a real hazard on this TUI, and what would the honest ordering be?
- The parent scanner reads only the tail of the transcript (12 MiB budget). A killed notification older than the window is missed and the row falls back to mtime → stalled after 15 min. Acceptable?
- Does adding a key to `counts` break any consumer you can think of besides a strict-decoding phone (which is now optional)?
- Anything that makes `stopped` misleading: e.g. an agent the user stopped and then resumed, still running, whose last notification was `killed`?
