VERDICT: DON'T SHIP

1. Set precedence is historically wrong. `completed → resume → killed` reports `finished`; multiple notifications per task ID make this a real hazard. The latest terminal notification must win.
2. Resume is broken. After `killed → resume`, the old killed record keeps the row `stopped` while the agent is actively running. Agent activity newer than the terminal event must override it.
3. Attribution is false. A desk-initiated stop also produces `<status>killed</status>`, so precedence selects `stopped-by-user`; `stopped-by-desk` is effectively hidden. Use `stopped` without actor unless independently known.
4. Tail truncation converts a known terminal stop into `stalled`, falsely implying an unhealthy live agent. Persist/index terminal state, or report `unknown` when absence cannot be established from a truncated transcript.
5. Compatibility risk is broader than `counts.stopped`: older clients with a closed `state` enum can fail decoding any row containing `stopped`. Roll out enum-tolerant clients before emitting the new state.
6. Clients summing only the original four count fields will undercount rows even if JSON decoding succeeds.
7. Parent unreadable outranking confirmed desk memory makes a directly observed stop become `unknown`; use the memory when valid, while marking its source/confidence.
